        "counterexample": "Frozen source supports the claim on its face: can_read(requester, owner, public=False) returns `public or bool(requester)`, which never compares requester to owner. Calling can_read(\"mallory\", \"alice\", public=False) yields True, granting a non-owner authenticated requester read access to a private document, contrary to the README rule that access is permitted only for public documents or a requester equal to owner.\n\nThe executed validation, however, does not test that source. The only generated file is generated/check.py, whose body is `# /source/sample.py` plus `print(json.dumps({\"claim_present\": sys.argv[1] == \"yes\"}))`. It never imports or reads sample.py; the sample.py reference exists solely as a comment. Baseline passes argv \"yes\" and counterexample passes argv \"no\", so claim_present is a pure echo of the command line. Both runs would produce identical exits and observations if can_read were rewritten to the correct `public or (bool(requester) and requester == owner)`, or if sample.py were deleted entirely. The controls therefore distinguish nothing about the claim versus its negation: the harness is a print-only, argv-echo shim with no source binding, so the executed distinction is invalid even though the underlying finding is correct by inspection of the supplied frozen source.",
